<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Why farm buildings need their own approach</h2>
<p>Agricultural structures are a big part of Nebraska’s insured property, and they are some of the hardest to value. Post-frame buildings, steel grain bins, hog and cattle barns and Quonset-style shops each use different materials and repair methods. Many estimates price them with residential line items that do not fit.</p>
<h2>Losses we see</h2>
<ul>
<li>Steel panels and trim dented by hail across a whole building</li>
<li>Pole barns racked or collapsed by straight-line winds</li>
<li>Grain bins torn from foundations or crushed by wind</li>
<li>Roof collapse from heavy snow or ice load</li>
<li>Fire damage to shops, hay storage and livestock buildings</li>
</ul>
<h2>Policy questions that change the value</h2>
<p>Farm policies often schedule each building with its own limit and valuation method. Some outbuildings are insured at actual cash value, others at replacement cost, and some at a stated amount. We start with the schedule so each structure is valued on the right basis, and we flag buildings that may be underinsured before the dispute goes further.</p>
<h2>Pricing that fits rural Nebraska</h2>
<p>Repair costs on a farm include travel, equipment such as lifts and cranes, specialty panel profiles and sometimes the cost of moving stored grain or machinery out of the way. We price those items explicitly so they are not lost in a generic estimate.</p>

<h2>Questions about this service</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Are grain bins covered under a homeowners policy?</summary><div class="a"><p>Usually not. Grain bins and most farm buildings are insured under a farm or ranch policy, often with each structure listed separately. Check your schedule.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can dented steel panels be covered if they still keep water out?</summary><div class="a"><p>It depends on the policy. Some farm and commercial forms exclude cosmetic damage to metal; others do not. The exact wording decides it.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you travel to the Sandhills and Panhandle?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. We work statewide, including ranches in the Sandhills and farms in the Panhandle, and we schedule rural inspections in clusters to keep costs down.</p></div></details></div>
